#doc(title: [Figures in the flow], subject: "Images of every format, sized and captioned")

= Waves on a string

A transverse wave on a string is drawn below as a graph of displacement against distance (@fig:wave). Its amplitude is the height of a crest above the axis.

#figure[#image("assets/wave.png", alt: "A sine wave over one and a half wavelengths, drawn on two axes")][A displacement–distance graph] <fig:wave>

The circuit in @fig:circuit is drawn as an SVG file, so it stays sharp at any zoom; the field in @fig:field comes from a PDF file.

#figure[#image("assets/circuit.svg", alt: "A cell, a resistor and a lamp in series", width: 60%)][A series circuit] <fig:circuit>

The forces on a block on a ramp are drawn in @fig:ramp, with a shadow under the block and arrowheads that follow their lines.

#figure[#image("assets/ramp.svg", alt: "A block on a ramp, with its weight straight down, the normal force perpendicular to the ramp and the component of the weight down the slope", width: 70%)][Forces on a ramp] <fig:ramp>

#figure[#image("assets/field.pdf", alt: "Field lines curving from a positive charge on the left to a negative charge on the right")][The field between two charges] <fig:field>

Colour swatches from a printer's PDF keep their calibrated colours, spot colours and blends (@fig:spectrum).

#figure[#image("assets/spectrum.pdf", alt: "Five colour swatches in calibrated and Lab colour, two swatches of a spot colour, and two colour blends, one through the spot colour and one from blue to yellow", width: 80%)][Colour swatches and blends] <fig:spectrum>

= Pictures

A photograph is kept as the JPEG it is:

#figure[#image("assets/photo.jpg", alt: "A yellow disc on a blue and green gradient", width: 4cm)][A photograph]

Small pictures can stand in a line of text, such as a marker #image("assets/marker.webp", alt: "a red disc", width: 8pt) or a spring #image("assets/spring.gif", alt: "a zigzag spring", width: 24pt) between words.
